1.3.0 | 2026-10-19

  * New: non-TLS sessions on TLS ports (nontls.zeek; categories
    NONTLS_TLS_PORT_SESSION / NONTLS_TLS_PORT_BEACON). Judges only sessions
    observed from their start (client SYN + server SYN-ACK), with no
    content gaps, data in both directions and no protocol analyzer
    confirmed, to rare destinations, on nontls_ports (default 443/tcp).
    Alerts need >= 2 evidence items including an external corroborator
    (intel / compromised host / cadence) and a strong behaviour (intel /
    cadence / exfil). Replaces the removed encrypted-tunnel detector, whose
    false positives came from sessions joined mid-stream. Exclusions apply
    as for TLS, plus allowlisted domains via DNS answers
    (nontls_dns_exclusions). Never marks hosts compromised.
  * Resumed connections in the tunnel and reverse-flow detectors are now
    judged on the certificate verdict of the last full handshake to the
    same server IP, port and SNI (cert_cache_enabled). Previously they lost
    the valid-cert suppressor and the bad-cert corroborators.
  * New label-only indicator self_signed_or_forged_cert (self-signed, or a
    forged issuer detected via a missing/self-referential Authority Key
    Identifier). Adds no confidence and is not a corroborator.
  * New btest: nontls-and-certs.

1.2.0 | 2026-09-25

  Rebuilt on 1.0 (66e180f). Changes that reduce noise or cost are on by
  default; changes that increase sensitivity are opt-in or strictly gated.
  (The 1.1.x "loss of detection" report was a test-harness issue, not the
  package; the real issue was increased alert volume.)

  * New corroboration gate. Slow beacons (median interval >= 12 min, newly
    trackable) alert only at confidence >= 0.85 AND with >= 1 independent
    corroborator (certificate, SNI, TLS-stack rarity or threat intel —
    beacon_corroborating_indicators). Timing/shape tags, informational tags
    and host-state escalations never count. Tagged slow_beacon.
    beacon_min_corroborators (default 0) extends the requirement to every
    beacon for gradual tightening.
  * Host-compromise / payload-staging cluster relay split out into
    cluster_share_host_state (default OFF — it raises alert volume).
  * Defaults: candidate_admission_enabled=T, eager_popular_eviction=F,
    safe_ip_learning_enabled=T, cluster_share_state=T (popularity and
    safe-IP sharing), legacy_flow_lifecycle=F (slow tracking, gated),
    flowkey_resumption_invariant=F (opt-in), cluster_share_host_state=F.
  * Beacon trace lines now show tier, confidence vs required, and
    corroborator count vs required.

1.1.2 | 2026-09-24

  * SAFE DEFAULTS after a detection regression was reported in 1.1.0/1.1.1:
    every 1.1 behaviour change is now individually switchable and ALL
    default to the stable 1.0 behaviour, so the package can be deployed
    safely and each change validated on its own:
      candidate_admission_enabled   = F   (1.0: first contact tracked)
      flowkey_resumption_invariant  = F   (1.0: full-JA4 flow key)
      legacy_flow_lifecycle         = T   (1.0 abandonment rule) [new]
      eager_popular_eviction        = T   (1.0 eviction scan)    [new]
      safe_ip_learning_enabled      = F
      cluster_share_state           = F
  * New trace_orig_hosts option: every gate decision for a traced
    originator (skip reason, admission, abandonment, beacon evaluation and
    final confidence) is written to reporter.log.
  * Fixed stale detect.zeek header advertising a non-existent
    evaluate_sparse slow-beacon detector.

1.1.1 | 2026-09-24

  * Fixed a detection regression introduced in 1.1.0: cluster-wide
    popularity bypassed destinations far too aggressively.
    - The aggregator's distinct-client set used &write_expire, so it never
      reset while new clients kept arriving — a one-way ratchet in which any
      destination touched by 4 hosts over any number of days became popular
      and was dropped from analysis. It now uses &create_expire (a rolling
      24 h window), matching the worker-side dest_client_count.
    - The estate-wide count reused popular_dest_threshold (3), which was
      tuned against per-worker traffic slices. It now has its own
      cluster_popular_dest_threshold (default 20), so a C2 beaconing from a
      small cluster of infected hosts stays visible. Per-worker popularity is
      unchanged.

1.1.0 | 2026-09-23

  Detection accuracy
  * Fixed: beacons with an interval of ~13 minutes or longer could never be
    detected. Flows with fewer than 6 samples were abandoned after 1 hour and
    all flow state was hard-expired 6 hours after creation, so slow beacons
    were wiped before collecting enough samples (the "sparse_state path" the
    code referred to did not exist). Abandonment is now silence-based: a flow
    is discarded only after being silent for beacon_max_interval (4 h), and
    flow state uses idle expiry so an active beacon is never wiped mid-life.
    Beacons from 13 minutes to 4 hours are now detected; faster beacons are
    detected at exactly the same point as before.
  * Fixed: a TLS 1.3 beacon mixing full and resumed handshakes was split into
    two flows (resumption adds pre_shared_key, changing the JA4 extension
    count and hash). Flows are now grouped by a resumption-invariant key
    (flow_fp). Rarity, pivot and intel matching still use the full JA3/JA4.

  Exclusions / false positives
  * Allowlist propagation to SNI-less connections: when a connection is
    bypassed on an allowlisted SNI, its server IP is learned; later SNI-less,
    CONNECT-less connections to that IP inherit the bypass. Different SNIs to
    the same IP are still analysed; proxy IPs are never learned.
  * Trailing-dot FQDNs are normalised before allowlist matching.
  * Added ESET's other vendor-owned apex domains (e5.sk, eset.eu, eset.sk,
    esetsoftware.com) alongside eset.com / eset.systems.

  Cluster
  * New cluster state sharing (cluster_share_state, default on). Popularity
    is counted cluster-wide (new destination/client pairs routed to one
    aggregator per destination by rendezvous hashing; flips broadcast to all
    workers). Host-compromise, payload-staging and learned-safe-IP verdicts
    are relayed to every worker. Uses Cluster::publish on Zeek 7.1+ and
    Broker::publish on 7.0. Inert in standalone mode.
  * README documents the load-balancing requirement: the hash must keep a
    source/destination IP pair on one worker, or beacon samples are split.

  Performance
  * Two-tier admission: a first contact allocates only a timestamp; full flow
    state is created only when the host/destination/stack recurs within
    beacon_max_interval.
  * Popularity flips no longer scan the whole flow_state table (lazy
    eviction via idle expiry).

  Deprecated (still accepted so existing redefs load): track_abandon_after,
  flow_state_expiry.

  Testing
  * Added exclusions-and-keys btest: trailing-dot and vendor-apex allowlist
    matching, safe-IP learning, resumption-invariant flow keys, and cluster
    sharing inert in standalone mode.

1.0.0 | 2026-07-28

  Initial public release. Summary of the major capabilities and fixes
  developed prior to this release (full history in git log).

  Core behavioural detectors
  * Periodic SSL beaconing detection (full-handshake and session-resumption
    pinned), with jitter-tier classification (tight / jittered / chaotic)
    driven by MAD and Bowley skewness.
  * Long-lived tunnel / keep-alive detection, independent of SNI.
  * Reverse-flow / interactive-shell (RAT) detection via both SPL-based
    command-then-result timing and SPL-independent byte-shape heuristics.
  * Tunnelled-protocol-inside-TLS detection (fixed-frame / binary-burst
    inner protocols).
  * Living-off-trusted-sites pivot detection (rare client fingerprint to a
    popular CDN/platform).
  * Exfiltration escalation ladder tracking cumulative upload volume on a
    confirmed C2 channel.
  * Payload-staging / stage-transition correlation across channels on the
    same host.

  Statistical confirmation signals (all bounded corroborators layered on
  top of the behavioural detectors above; none of these create or gate a
  detection on their own)
  * Payload-size Peak Density Ratio: rescues a beacon whose timing looks
    evasive but whose response size is mechanically fixed.
  * Inter-quartile timing spread (IQR), judged RELATIVE to the interval:
    confirms bounded/proportionate jitter versus genuine chaos.
  * Inter-arrival-time entropy (relative-binned Shannon entropy): confirms
    predictable timing independent of dispersion.
  * Lag-1 autocorrelation: detects patterned (period-2 alternating)
    sleeps that inflate jitter while remaining mechanical, and prevents
    premature abandonment of such flows.
  * C2 lifecycle transition memory: recognises a channel that has shown
    BOTH a quiet (beacon/tunnel) phase and a reverse-flow phase, in either
    order — the hello/recon-then-quiet or established-C2-then-hands-on
    pattern seen in ransomware backdoors and APT footholds.

  False-positive defences
  * Fan-out prevalence gating: many internal hosts to one destination is
    treated as a shared service, not single-host C2.
  * Immediate-upload rejection: distinguishes a real low-and-slow
    interactive shell from high-rate media/WebRTC/backup uploads.
  * Reverse-flow RAT corroboration gate: an interactive-shell byte shape
    only earns the RAT label and its confidence when the flow
    independently looks like C2 (suspect/invalid cert, intel hit, tracked
    fingerprint, compromised host, or a lifecycle transition). A valid
    commercial certificate with an HTTPS-looking inner protocol no longer
    causes an endpoint-agent/telemetry flow to be mislabelled as a RAT.
  * Operator allowlist entries are ABSOLUTE: nothing (including a
    self-signed or non-matching certificate) can revoke an explicit
    allowlist match. Domain fronting remains covered via
    trusted_pivot_suffixes (kept under full analysis) and cert/SNI
    mismatch scoring, not via revoking the bypass.
  * JA4-primary fingerprinting with a browser-shape classifier and a
    cipher-hash learning mechanism, so Chromium/BoringSSL-family clients
    collapse to one identity regardless of extension-count churn.
  * Fixed: popular_dests now uses &write_expire (not &create_expire) so an
    actively-used popular destination stays popular instead of silently
    expiring and having to re-earn popularity from zero every
    popular_dest_expiry window.
  * Fixed: a reporter error that fired once per destination per popularity
    flip when flow state was indexed after eviction.
  * Removed the SSL-independent encrypted-tunnel (non-TLS-over-443)
    detector from this package — it is out of scope for a TLS/SSL-focused
    detector and belongs in a future dedicated TCP/multi-protocol C2
    package (logic preserved for that purpose).

  Testing
  * Added unit test coverage for the statistical confirmation signals
    (peak density, IQR spread, IAT entropy, lag-1 autocorrelation, Bowley
    skewness, quantile interpolation).
  * Added unit test coverage for the C2 lifecycle transition memory,
    including both phase orderings and negative cases (single repeated
    phase, unrelated channel).
  * Added unit test coverage for host-compromise correlation, payload
    staging, tracked-C2-fingerprint pivot matching, and destination
    fan-out/popularity tracking (a regression guard for the &write_expire
    fix above).
  * Added unit test coverage for the absolute-allowlist behaviour (suffix
    and apex matching, case-insensitivity) and JA4 browser-shape
    classification against real Chromium/BoringSSL fingerprints.
